# =============================================================================
# .dockerignore — Tells Docker which files to EXCLUDE from the build context
# =============================================================================
# When you run "docker build", Docker sends ALL files in the directory to the
# Docker daemon (the "build context"). This file works like .gitignore — it
# prevents unnecessary or sensitive files from being included.
#
# Why does this matter?
#   1. Smaller build context = faster builds (less data to transfer)
#   2. Prevents secrets (.env files) from accidentally ending up in images
#   3. Avoids cache-busting (if irrelevant files change, Docker won't rebuild)
# =============================================================================

# Python bytecode cache — generated at runtime, never needed in the image.
# Including these would waste space and could cause cross-platform issues
# (bytecode compiled on your Mac/PC won't work in the Linux container).
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Environment variable files — these often contain secrets like database
# passwords and API keys. NEVER bake these into a Docker image! Instead,
# pass them at runtime via docker-compose environment: or Kubernetes Secrets.
.env

# Git directory — contains your entire repo history, hooks, and config.
# This can be huge and is completely irrelevant inside a container.
.git/

# Virtual environment — your local Python venv has platform-specific binaries
# (e.g., compiled for Windows/Mac). The container installs its own deps
# via pip inside the Linux-based image, so your local venv is useless here.
venv/

# Markdown/documentation files — nice for humans reading GitHub, but the
# container only needs runnable code. Excluding these keeps the image lean.
*.md
